  "body_html": "<h1>Self-Dual Lattice Function</h1>\n<p>This is the shared foundation layer node for self-dual lattice functions.\nIt should be used by both epipelagic and supercuspidal nodes instead of\nthe older <a class=\"node-ref\" data-node-id=\"epipelagic_theta.self_dual_lattice_function\" href=\"../epipelagic_theta/epipelagic_theta_self_dual_lattice_function.html\"><code>epipelagic_theta.self_dual_lattice_function</code></a>.</p>\n<p>Let \\(V\\) carry a nondegenerate sesquilinear form. For an \\(o_D\\)-lattice\n\\(L\\subseteq V\\), its hermitian dual is\n\\[\nL^\\#=\\{v\\in V : \\langle v,L\\rangle_V\\subseteq \\mathfrak p_D\\}.\n\\]\nThis knowledge base uses the conductor-\\(\\mathfrak p\\) convention for the\nfixed additive character \\(\\psi\\). Some sources denote the same dual lattice\nby \\(L^\\ast\\); the notation \\(L^\\vee\\) is avoided here.</p>\n<p>For a lattice function \\(L\\), the dual lattice function is defined by\n\\[\n(L^\\#)_s=(L_{(-s)+})^\\#.\n\\]\nThe lattice function is self-dual when \\(L=L^\\#\\). Under the Bruhat-Tits identification for classical groups, points in the building correspond to self-dual lattice functions.</p>",
  "proof_html": null,
